    "content": " Tulum, located on Mexico's Caribbean coast in Quintana Roo, is a renowned tourist destination famous for its well-preserved, cliff-top Mayan ruins, stunning white-sand beaches, and bohemian-chic vibe featuring cenotes and jungle yoga. It is split into the beachside hotel zone and the more affordable town center. As of early 2026, it is experiencing lower prices and a quieter, more relaxed atmosphere following a downturn in tourism. Key Attractions & Activities Tulum Archaeological Zone: Pre-Columbian Mayan ruins overlooking the sea, formerly a major port. Cenotes: Popular underground caves and sinkholes for swimming, snorkeling, and diving, such as Dos Ojos or Sac Actun. Beaches & Beach Clubs: While some beaches are now part of the protected Parque del Jaguar (requires entrance fee) or private beach clubs, popular spots for relaxing include the beach area of the town. Nature Reserves: Punta Laguna Nature Reserve for wildlife viewing. Nightlife: Known for its vibrant, upscale beach bars and nightlife, including Papaya Playa Project, Gitano, and Casa Jaguar. Travel Tips Best Time to Visit: December to March offers the best weather. Getting Around: The town is flat and ideal for navigating by bicycle. Seaweed Note: Significant sargassum (seaweed) can appear on the beaches between April and October. Accommodation: The hotel zone is luxurious and pricey, while the town offers more reasonably priced options and authentic restaurants.",
